## Bug found & fixed (src/impl/util.js)

The leap-year helper `isLeapYear` was already correct (`year % 4 === 0 && (year % 100 !== 0 || year % 400 === 0)`). The real bug was in **`objToLocalTS`** (the helper that undoes JS's 19XX remap for years 0–99):

```diff
@@ -166,7 +169,10 @@ export function objToLocalTS(obj) {
   // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
   if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
     d = new Date(d);
-    d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
+    // set the month and day again, this is necessary because year 2000 is a leap year, but year 100 is not
+    // so if obj.year is in 99, but obj.day makes it roll over into year 100,
+    // the calculations done by Date.UTC are using year 2000 - which is incorrect
+    d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day);
   }
   return +d;
 }
```

**Why:** for e.g. `0099-12-31 + 60 days`, `adjustTime` builds `{year:99, month:12, day:91}`. `Date.UTC(99,11,91)` normalizes using year **2000** (a leap year) → 2000-02-29; subtracting 1900 from 2000 gives 100, but the month/day were computed with year 2000's leap structure, so year 100 (not leap) gained a phantom Feb 29 and every date after Feb 28 shifted one day early. Re-setting `(year, month, day)` makes normalization use year 100's correct structure. This is exactly the upstream luxon 1.11.1 fix ("Fix DateTime#plus() when spanning across AD 100").

## Verification

- **Comprehensive differential test** (y=90..110 × all months/days, `plus/minus` days 1..100, months 1..13, years 1..3 vs a proleptic-Gregorian reference): **BEFORE 820 mismatches → AFTER 0**.
- **Throwaway test** `test/datetime/tmpverify.test.js`, named exactly `DateTime#plus works across the 100 barrier when passing through February`, asserting the task's cases (`local(100,1,31)+1mo = 0100-02-28`; `local(100,2,28)+1d = 0100-03-01`; `local(104,2,28)+1d = 0104-02-29`; `100.isInLeapYear === false`; `104.isInLeapYear === true`) **plus** the genu

